{"id": "517697ff-738a-4470-a376-d6f956dec75a", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T08:32:36.840628", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "3c0621b0-74f9-487e-a389-69d421024950", "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "### Proposed Approach for Experimental Design: Evaluating Period Settings on PDF Model for ETTm1\n\n#### 1. Objective and Scope\nThe goal is to systematically evaluate how varying period settings (hyperparameters governing periodic decomposition, reshaping, or frequency modeling) influence the forecasting accuracy and robustness of the PDF model on the ETTm1 benchmark dataset across multiple standard prediction horizons.\n\n#### 2. Key Experimental Dimensions & Variables\n- **Independent Variable (Period Settings)**:\n  - **Domain-aligned / Physical Periods**: Based on ETTm1's 15-minute sampling interval ($4\\text{ steps/hour}$):\n    - Daily periodicity: $P = 96$ ($24 \\times 4$ steps).\n    - Half-day periodicity: $P = 48$ ($12 \\times 4$ steps).\n    - Weekly periodicity: $P = 672$ ($7 \\times 96$ steps).\n  - **Mismatched / Cross-dataset Periods**:\n    - Period values common in hourly datasets (e.g., $P = 24$, corresponding to 6 hours on 15-minute data).\n    - Sub-optimal/arbitrary periods (e.g., $P \\in \\{12, 32, 128, 192, 384\\}$).\n  - **Multi-Period vs. Single-Period Configurations**: Testing single period values versus multi-period lists/hierarchies (e.g., $[96]$, $[48, 96]$, $[96, 672]$), if supported by the model architecture.\n- **Fixed & Controlled Variables**:\n  - Lookback context length (e.g., $L = 96$ or $L = 336$, held identical across all period variations).\n  - Model capacity (hidden dimensions, number of layers, decomposition heads).\n  - Training hyper-parameters (optimizer, learning rate schedule, batch size, loss function like MSE, early stopping criteria).\n  - Dataset split (standard 7:1:1 or 12/4/4 month temporal split, zero-leakage standard normalization fitted only on training set).\n- **Forecasting Horizons**:\n  - Standard benchmark prediction lengths: $T \\in \\{96, 192, 336, 720\\}$ steps ($1, 2, 3.5, 7.5$ days).\n- **Evaluation Metrics & Verification**:\n  - Mean Squared Error (MSE) and Mean Absolute Error (MAE).\n  - Statistical significance: 5 repeated runs with varying random seeds per setting to report mean and standard deviation.\n\n#### 3. Consequential Choices & Analysis Strategy\n- **Ablation Matrix**: Compare model performance as period setting deviates from the true intrinsic period (96) to quantify sensitivity to period misspecification.\n- **Computational Efficiency Analysis**: Track training time, parameter count, and inference latency across different period values (since period size often impacts 2D tensor dimensions or kernel sizes).\n\nPlease let me know if this proposed approach aligns with your expectations, or if you would like any specific period ranges, baseline comparisons, or architectural variants prioritized before developing the full experimental design.", "evidence": "Workspace inspection revealed an isolated design environment (task.json indicating a design-only task with track.md noting that compute/repositories are not required). In ETTm1, data points are recorded at 15-minute intervals, meaning the daily seasonal cycle corresponds to 96 steps and weekly to 672 steps, which provides the physical ground-truth for testing period settings.", "assumptions": ["ETTm1 follows standard benchmark preprocessing (15-minute sampling frequency, 7 multivariate channels, standardized train/validation/test temporal split).", "Standard forecasting horizons T in {96, 192, 336, 720} are used with a fixed input lookback window L (e.g., L=96 or L=336).", "The PDF model takes explicit period parameter(s) (either as a scalar period, kernel/patch size, or list of periods) that govern periodic feature extraction."], "uncertainties": ["Specific architectural implementation details of the PDF model (e.g., whether period defines a 1D decomposition kernel size, 2D intra-/inter-period reshaping, or frequency-domain filtering).", "Whether multi-period configurations (lists/tuples) or only scalar period settings are under investigation.", "Whether specific baseline models (e.g., TimesNet, DLinear, PatchTST) should be integrated as reference comparisons."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call_995427__thought__", "tool_call": {"id": "call_995427__thought__", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"answer\": \"### Proposed Approach for Experimental Design: Evaluating Period Settings on PDF Model for ETTm1\\n\\n#### 1. Objective and Scope\\nThe goal is to systematically evaluate how varying period settings (hyperparameters governing periodic decomposition, reshaping, or frequency modeling) influence the forecasting accuracy and robustness of the PDF model on the ETTm1 benchmark dataset across multiple standard prediction horizons.\\n\\n#### 2. Key Experimental Dimensions & Variables\\n- **Independent Variable (Period Settings)**:\\n  - **Domain-aligned / Physical Periods**: Based on ETTm1's 15-minute sampling interval ($4\\\\text{ steps/hour}$):\\n    - Daily periodicity: $P = 96$ ($24 \\\\times 4$ steps).\\n    - Half-day periodicity: $P = 48$ ($12 \\\\times 4$ steps).\\n    - Weekly periodicity: $P = 672$ ($7 \\\\times 96$ steps).\\n  - **Mismatched / Cross-dataset Periods**:\\n    - Period values common in hourly datasets (e.g., $P = 24$, corresponding to 6 hours on 15-minute data).\\n    - Sub-optimal/arbitrary periods (e.g., $P \\\\in \\\\{12, 32, 128, 192, 384\\\\}$).\\n  - **Multi-Period vs. Single-Period Configurations**: Testing single period values versus multi-period lists/hierarchies (e.g., $[96]$, $[48, 96]$, $[96, 672]$), if supported by the model architecture.\\n- **Fixed & Controlled Variables**:\\n  - Lookback context length (e.g., $L = 96$ or $L = 336$, held identical across all period variations).\\n  - Model capacity (hidden dimensions, number of layers, decomposition heads).\\n  - Training hyper-parameters (optimizer, learning rate schedule, batch size, loss function like MSE, early stopping criteria).\\n  - Dataset split (standard 7:1:1 or 12/4/4 month temporal split, zero-leakage standard normalization fitted only on training set).\\n- **Forecasting Horizons**:\\n  - Standard benchmark prediction lengths: $T \\\\in \\\\{96, 192, 336, 720\\\\}$ steps ($1, 2, 3.5, 7.5$ days).\\n- **Evaluation Metrics & Verification**:\\n  - Mean Squared Error (MSE) and Mean Absolute Error (MAE).\\n  - Statistical significance: 5 repeated runs with varying random seeds per setting to report mean and standard deviation.\\n\\n#### 3. Consequential Choices & Analysis Strategy\\n- **Ablation Matrix**: Compare model performance as period setting deviates from the true intrinsic period (96) to quantify sensitivity to period misspecification.\\n- **Computational Efficiency Analysis**: Track training time, parameter count, and inference latency across different period values (since period size often impacts 2D tensor dimensions or kernel sizes).\\n\\nPlease let me know if this proposed approach aligns with your expectations, or if you would like any specific period ranges, baseline comparisons, or architectural variants prioritized before developing the full experimental design.\", \"assumptions\": [\"ETTm1 follows standard benchmark preprocessing (15-minute sampling frequency, 7 multivariate channels, standardized train/validation/test temporal split).\", \"Standard forecasting horizons T in {96, 192, 336, 720} are used with a fixed input lookback window L (e.g., L=96 or L=336).\", \"The PDF model takes explicit period parameter(s) (either as a scalar period, kernel/patch size, or list of periods) that govern periodic feature extraction.\"], \"summary\": \"Propose approach and key dimensions for evaluating period settings of PDF on ETTm1\", \"evidence\": \"Workspace inspection revealed an isolated design environment (task.json indicating a design-only task with track.md noting that compute/repositories are not required). In ETTm1, data points are recorded at 15-minute intervals, meaning the daily seasonal cycle corresponds to 96 steps and weekly to 672 steps, which provides the physical ground-truth for testing period settings.\", \"stage\": \"plan\", \"uncertainties\": [\"Specific architectural implementation details of the PDF model (e.g., whether period defines a 1D decomposition kernel size, 2D intra-/inter-period reshaping, or frequency-domain filtering).\", \"Whether multi-period configurations (lists/tuples) or only scalar period settings are under investigation.\", \"Whether specific baseline models (e.g., TimesNet, DLinear, PatchTST) should be integrated as reference comparisons.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "dObUaojiIOmQ9LsPy-eN4A4", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 20}
