{"id": "5e223b3e-874d-4be5-ba01-2b1daf2b02ce", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T08:32:43.669192", "source": "user", "parent_id": "ca150e5c-de69-458a-b2d5-c933f0764366", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"The proposed approach is sound and well aligned with the benchmark context and 15-minute sampling characteristics of ETTm1. Please proceed to develop the complete final experiment design. Ensure your deliverable thoroughly covers: 1) theoretical rationale and hypothesis formulation regarding period settings in PDF, 2) exact experimental matrix and control variables, 3) detailed protocol and evaluation metrics, 4) statistical and sensitivity analysis plan, and 5) practical feasibility, computational budget, and potential threats to validity. Note that this is a design-only task, so do not claim or fabricate executed results.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 22}
{"id": "7b74e793-0d00-4783-beb6-bf4f6bc65ea8", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T08:33:33.968668", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "5e223b3e-874d-4be5-ba01-2b1daf2b02ce", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experimental Design: Evaluating the Effect of Period Settings on the PDF Model for ETTm1\n\n---\n\n## 1. Theoretical Rationale and Hypothesis Formulation\n\n### 1.1 Architectural Role of Period Settings in PDF\nIn periodic time series modeling (such as the PDF model, which leverages periodic decomposition, intra-period/inter-period 2D representations, or periodic convolution/filtering), the **period setting ($P$)** serves as the fundamental structural prior governing how 1D temporal sequences are partitioned or decomposed:\n1. **Intra-Period Dynamics**: High-frequency intra-cycle variations (e.g., immediate thermal fluctuations, rapid load changes).\n2. **Inter-Period Dynamics**: Low-frequency inter-cycle trend patterns and seasonal drift across consecutive cycles.\n\nWhen the period hyperparameter $P$ matches the intrinsic physical cycle of the data-generating process, the model aligns its receptive field and structural operations with true recurring patterns, minimizing phase distortion and frequency aliasing.\n\n### 1.2 Physical and Frequency Characteristics of ETTm1\nThe ETTm1 (Electricity Transformer Temperature - 15-minute sampling frequency) dataset records 7 oil and load features ($HUFL, HULL, MUFL, MULL, LUFL, LULL, OT$) at 15-minute intervals. Consequently:\n- **1 hour** = 4 time steps ($4 \\times 15\\text{ min} = 60\\text{ min}$).\n- **1 day (diurnal cycle)** = 96 time steps ($24 \\times 4$).\n- **1 week (weekly cycle)** = 672 time steps ($7 \\times 96$).\n\nIn prior literature, models evaluated on hourly ETT datasets (ETTh1, ETTh2) use a daily period of $P = 24$. If a researcher naively transfers $P = 24$ to ETTm1, the model assumes a 6-hour cycle, causing substantial phase misalignment.\n\n### 1.3 Hypotheses Formulation\n- **Hypothesis 1 (True Period Optimality)**: Setting the primary period to the true diurnal cycle ($P = 96$) minimizes Mean Squared Error (MSE) and Mean Absolute Error (MAE) across standard forecasting horizons compared to mismatched periods.\n- **Hypothesis 2 (Harmonic Graceful Degradation)**: Harmonic multiples and sub-multiples of the diurnal cycle ($P \\in \\{48, 192\\}$) will maintain competitive forecasting performance with only moderate degradation compared to $P = 96$, because they preserve integer-ratio phase alignment. In contrast, incommensurate or non-harmonic periods (e.g., $P \\in \\{32, 64, 80, 112\\}$) will suffer severe phase-aliasing and higher error.\n- **Hypothesis 3 (Cross-Dataset Period Misconfiguration Penalty)**: Using $P = 24$ (the hourly-dataset default) on 15-minute data will result in marked performance degradation due to cycle truncation (forcing a 6-hour artificial periodicity on a 24-hour physical phenomenon).\n- **Hypothesis 4 (Horizon-Dependent Period Hierarchy)**: For short horizons ($T = 96$), intra-day periodic fidelity ($P = 96$ or $P = 48$) dominates accuracy; for extended horizons ($T = 720$), multi-period configurations (e.g., combining daily $P = 96$ with weekly $P = 672$ or multi-day $P = 192$) will outperform any single-period setting by capturing long-range inter-cycle drift.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Experimental Matrix and Controlled Variables\n\n### 2.1 Dataset Configuration & Preprocessing\n- **Dataset**: ETTm1 (7 multivariate time series channels).\n- **Partitioning**: Chronological split standard for benchmark comparability:\n  - **Train**: First 12 months (34,560 time steps).\n  - **Validation**: Subsequent 4 months (11,520 time steps).\n  - **Test**: Final 4 months (11,520 time steps).\n- **Data Scaling**: Z-score standard normalization ($\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{norm}} = \\frac{\\mathbf{x} - \\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{\\text{train}}}{\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}_{\\text{train}} + \\epsilon}$), fitted strictly on the training partition to prevent lookahead data leakage.\n- **Task Modes**: Multivariate forecasting (predicting all 7 channels simultaneously) and Target Channel evaluation (measuring 'OT' accuracy).\n\n### 2.2 Independent Variable: Period Configurations ($P$)\nTo systematically explore the performance landscape, 12 discrete period configurations grouped into 4 distinct operational categories are tested:\n\n| Group | Configuration ID | Period Setting ($P$) | Theoretical Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **A. Physical Harmonics** | `P-96` *(Baseline)* | $P = 96$ | True ground-truth diurnal cycle (24 hours). |\n| | `P-48` | $P = 48$ | Sub-harmonic (12 hours / half-day). |\n| | `P-192` | $P = 192$ | Super-harmonic (48 hours / 2 days). |\n| | `P-24` | $P = 24$ | Hourly-dataset transfer baseline (6 hours on ETTm1). |\n| **B. Non-Harmonic Controls** | `P-32` | $P = 32$ | Incommensurate sub-daily (8 hours). |\n| | `P-64` | $P = 64$ | Incommensurate sub-daily (16 hours). |\n| | `P-80` | $P = 80$ | Near-daily incommensurate (20 hours; test sensitivity near $P=96$). |\n| | `P-112` | $P = 112$ | Near-daily incommensurate (28 hours; test sensitivity near $P=96$). |\n| | `P-144` | $P = 144$ | Arbitrary 1.5-day cycle (36 hours). |\n| **C. Multi-Period Combinations** | `P-Multi-A` | $[48, 96]$ | Dual-scale: half-day + full-day hierarchy. |\n| *(if multi-period supported)* | `P-Multi-B` | $[96, 192]$ | Dual-scale: full-day + two-day hierarchy. |\n| | `P-Multi-C` | $[24, 48, 96]$ | Triple-scale hierarchical decomposition. |\n| **D. Data-Driven Baseline** | `P-Auto` | Top-$k$ FFT / AutoCorr | Unsupervised periodicity extracted from training set. |\n\n### 2.3 Prediction Horizons ($T$) and Lookback Window ($L$)\n- **Lookback Context Window ($L$)**: Held fixed at $L = 336$ time steps (3.5 days), allowing sufficient historical cycles for all $P \\le 192$.\n- **Ablation Context Window**: $L = 672$ evaluated specifically when testing weekly periodicity $P = 672$.\n- **Prediction Horizons ($T$)**: Evaluated across the 4 standard long-term benchmarks:\n  $$T \\in \\{96, 192, 336, 720\\} \\text{ steps (corresponding to 24h, 48h, 84h, and 180h)}$$\n\n### 2.4 Controlled Variables\nTo isolate the effect of period settings, all other architectural and optimization parameters are held strictly invariant:\n- **Model Architecture**: Number of layers ($N = 2$), model feature dimension ($d_{model} = 64$ or default PDF dimension), dropout rate ($p = 0.1$).\n- **Optimizer**: Adam ($\\beta_1 = 0.9, \\beta_2 = 0.999$, weight decay $= 10^{-4}$).\n- **Learning Rate Schedule**: Initial learning rate $\\eta = 10^{-3}$, reduced via Cosine Annealing or ReduceLROnPlateau (factor $0.5$, patience $2$).\n- **Batch Size**: 32.\n- **Maximum Epochs & Early Stopping**: 20 epochs with early stopping on validation loss (patience $= 5$ epochs).\n- **Loss Function**: Mean Squared Error (MSE).\n- **Random Seeds**: 5 independent fixed random seeds ($\\{2021, 2022, 2023, 2024, 2025\\}$) across every experimental cell.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Detailed Protocol and Evaluation Metrics\n\n### 3.1 Step-by-Step Execution Protocol\n1. **Window Generation**: Construct input-target pairs $(\\mathbf{X}_{t-L+1:t}, \\mathbf{Y}_{t+1:t+T})$ using a rolling sliding window with step size 1 across the normalized split partitions.\n2. **Model Instantiation**: Initialize the PDF model with the designated period setting $P$ from the experimental matrix, fixing all other hyperparameters.\n3. **Training & Checkpointing**:\n   - Train on the training split using backpropagation with the MSE loss.\n   - Evaluate validation loss after each epoch. Save the model checkpoint that achieves the lowest validation MSE.\n   - Terminate training if validation MSE does not improve for 5 consecutive epochs.\n4. **Inference & Inverse Transformation**:\n   - Run inference on the unseen test split using the optimal checkpoint.\n   - Invert the standard normalization back to physical units: $\\hat{\\mathbf{Y}} = \\hat{\\mathbf{Y}}_{\\text{norm}} \\times \\boldsymbol{\\sigma}_{\\text{train}} + \\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{\\text{train}}$.\n5. **Metric Calculation**: Compute primary and secondary metrics on both normalized and unnormalized scales.\n\n### 3.2 Evaluation Metrics\n- **Mean Squared Error (MSE)**:\n  $$\\text{MSE} = \\frac{1}{T \\cdot D} \\sum_{i=1}^T \\sum_{j=1}^D (Y_{i,j} - \\hat{Y}_{i,j})^2$$\n- **Mean Absolute Error (MAE)**:\n  $$\\text{MAE} = \\frac{1}{T \\cdot D} \\sum_{i=1}^T \\sum_{j=1}^D |Y_{i,j} - \\hat{Y}_{i,j}|$$\n- **Relative Performance Change ($\\Delta \\text{Metric}_{\\%}$)** against the ground-truth baseline ($P = 96$):\n  $$\\Delta \\text{MSE}_{\\%}(P) = \\frac{\\text{MSE}_P - \\text{MSE}_{P=96}}{\\text{MSE}_{P=96}} \\times 100\\%$$\n- **Efficiency Metrics**: Average training wall-clock time per epoch (seconds), inference latency per batch (milliseconds), and peak GPU memory allocation (MB).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Statistical and Sensitivity Analysis Plan\n\n### 4.1 Statistical Aggregation & Significance Testing\n- **Summary Statistics**: Report the sample mean ($\\bar{\\mu}$) and sample standard deviation ($\\sigma$) across the 5 independent random seed runs for each $(P, T)$ combination.\n- **Hypothesis Testing**:\n  - Perform **paired two-sided t-tests** and **Wilcoxon signed-rank tests** comparing each period setting against the ground-truth baseline ($P = 96$) across all test windows.\n  - Apply the **Bonferroni-Holm adjustment** to control the family-wise error rate (FWER) across the multiple pairwise comparisons at significance threshold $\\alpha = 0.05$.\n\n### 4.2 Sensitivity Surface & Period Perturbation Analysis\n- **Period-Error Response Curves**: Plot MSE/MAE as a continuous function of $P$ across horizons $T \\in \\{96, 192, 336, 720\\}$ to examine whether the loss surface forms a local convex minimum at $P = 96$.\n- **Harmonic vs. Incommensurate Contrast**:\n  - Compute contrast statistics:\n    $$\\delta_{\\text{harmonic}} = \\text{MSE}_{\\text{harmonic}} - \\text{MSE}_{P=96}$$\n    $$\\delta_{\\text{non-harmonic}} = \\text{MSE}_{\\text{non-harmonic}} - \\text{MSE}_{P=96}$$\n  - Evaluate whether $\\delta_{\\text{harmonic}} < \\delta_{\\text{non-harmonic}}$ is statistically significant, confirming that integer-multiple phase alignment cushions performance loss.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Feasibility, Computational Budget, and Validity Threats\n\n### 5.1 Computational Budget & Feasibility\n- **Total Experimental Runs**:\n  - $12 \\text{ period settings} \\times 4 \\text{ prediction horizons } (96, 192, 336, 720) \\times 5 \\text{ random seeds} = 240 \\text{ training runs}$.\n- **Compute Estimation**:\n  - ETTm1 training set has $34,560$ steps; with batch size 32 and early stopping at ~10–15 epochs, each run requires approximately 3–4 minutes on a single modern GPU (e.g., NVIDIA RTX 3090, A100, or V100).\n  - Total computational cost is estimated at **12 to 16 GPU hours**, making the full matrix execution completely feasible within standard academic/industrial computing constraints.\n\n### 5.2 Potential Threats to Validity & Mitigation Strategies\n1. **Window Divisibility and Boundary Padding**:\n   - *Threat*: When the lookback window $L = 336$ is not an exact integer multiple of $P$ (e.g., $336 / 80 = 4.2$), models performing 2D reshaping must pad or truncate sequences, introducing edge artifacts.\n   - *Mitigation*: Standardize the padding mechanism (reflective or circular padding rather than zero padding) and record padding overhead to separate architectural truncation artifacts from genuine periodic modeling differences.\n2. **Hyperparameter Coupling**:\n   - *Threat*: The optimal learning rate or dropout might vary slightly depending on the period setting (e.g., larger periods creating larger 2D representations).\n   - *Mitigation*: Keep hyperparameters fixed at standard benchmark defaults to isolate the pure effect of the period prior, supplemented by a validation grid-search on learning rate $\\{10^{-4}, 10^{-3}\\}$ for the edge configurations ($P=24$ and $P=96$) to confirm that conclusions are not artifacts of sub-optimal learning rates.\n3. **Temporal Covariate Shift**:\n   - *Threat*: Seasonal variations across the 12/4/4 month splits (summer vs. winter transformer loads) could favor different periods in validation versus test sets.\n   - *Mitigation*: Ensure models are selected strictly via validation loss and evaluate test metrics over the entire 4-month continuous test period, reporting rolling-window consistency.\n4. **Non-Execution Notice**:\n   - This document defines the complete experimental design, protocols, matrix, and analytical formulations. In strict accordance with the task instructions, no experimental execution or simulated results are claimed.", "evidence": "Inspection of the workspace confirmed an isolated environment intended for design-only tasks without execution. ETTm1 dataset characteristics (15-min sampling, 96 steps/day, 672 steps/week) established the theoretical ground truth for evaluating period misalignment, harmonic ratios, and multi-period combinations.", "assumptions": ["The PDF model accepts explicit period settings or period candidate lists as configuration hyperparameters.", "The standard ETTm1 benchmark split (12/4/4 months) and 15-minute sampling rate (96 steps/day) are used.", "Computational resources for executing the design require approximately 12-16 single-GPU hours for 240 training runs."], "uncertainties": ["Specific internal tensor reshaping/decomposition operators within the proprietary or variant implementation of the PDF model (e.g., whether non-divisible windows use reflection padding, interpolation, or truncation)."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call_638803__thought__", "tool_call": {"id": "call_638803__thought__", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"evidence\": \"Inspection of the workspace confirmed an isolated environment intended for design-only tasks without execution. ETTm1 dataset characteristics (15-min sampling, 96 steps/day, 672 steps/week) established the theoretical ground truth for evaluating period misalignment, harmonic ratios, and multi-period combinations.\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"uncertainties\": [\"Specific internal tensor reshaping/decomposition operators within the proprietary or variant implementation of the PDF model (e.g., whether non-divisible windows use reflection padding, interpolation, or truncation).\"], \"answer\": \"# Experimental Design: Evaluating the Effect of Period Settings on the PDF Model for ETTm1\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Theoretical Rationale and Hypothesis Formulation\\n\\n### 1.1 Architectural Role of Period Settings in PDF\\nIn periodic time series modeling (such as the PDF model, which leverages periodic decomposition, intra-period/inter-period 2D representations, or periodic convolution/filtering), the **period setting ($P$)** serves as the fundamental structural prior governing how 1D temporal sequences are partitioned or decomposed:\\n1. **Intra-Period Dynamics**: High-frequency intra-cycle variations (e.g., immediate thermal fluctuations, rapid load changes).\\n2. **Inter-Period Dynamics**: Low-frequency inter-cycle trend patterns and seasonal drift across consecutive cycles.\\n\\nWhen the period hyperparameter $P$ matches the intrinsic physical cycle of the data-generating process, the model aligns its receptive field and structural operations with true recurring patterns, minimizing phase distortion and frequency aliasing.\\n\\n### 1.2 Physical and Frequency Characteristics of ETTm1\\nThe ETTm1 (Electricity Transformer Temperature - 15-minute sampling frequency) dataset records 7 oil and load features ($HUFL, HULL, MUFL, MULL, LUFL, LULL, OT$) at 15-minute intervals. Consequently:\\n- **1 hour** = 4 time steps ($4 \\\\times 15\\\\text{ min} = 60\\\\text{ min}$).\\n- **1 day (diurnal cycle)** = 96 time steps ($24 \\\\times 4$).\\n- **1 week (weekly cycle)** = 672 time steps ($7 \\\\times 96$).\\n\\nIn prior literature, models evaluated on hourly ETT datasets (ETTh1, ETTh2) use a daily period of $P = 24$. If a researcher naively transfers $P = 24$ to ETTm1, the model assumes a 6-hour cycle, causing substantial phase misalignment.\\n\\n### 1.3 Hypotheses Formulation\\n- **Hypothesis 1 (True Period Optimality)**: Setting the primary period to the true diurnal cycle ($P = 96$) minimizes Mean Squared Error (MSE) and Mean Absolute Error (MAE) across standard forecasting horizons compared to mismatched periods.\\n- **Hypothesis 2 (Harmonic Graceful Degradation)**: Harmonic multiples and sub-multiples of the diurnal cycle ($P \\\\in \\\\{48, 192\\\\}$) will maintain competitive forecasting performance with only moderate degradation compared to $P = 96$, because they preserve integer-ratio phase alignment. In contrast, incommensurate or non-harmonic periods (e.g., $P \\\\in \\\\{32, 64, 80, 112\\\\}$) will suffer severe phase-aliasing and higher error.\\n- **Hypothesis 3 (Cross-Dataset Period Misconfiguration Penalty)**: Using $P = 24$ (the hourly-dataset default) on 15-minute data will result in marked performance degradation due to cycle truncation (forcing a 6-hour artificial periodicity on a 24-hour physical phenomenon).\\n- **Hypothesis 4 (Horizon-Dependent Period Hierarchy)**: For short horizons ($T = 96$), intra-day periodic fidelity ($P = 96$ or $P = 48$) dominates accuracy; for extended horizons ($T = 720$), multi-period configurations (e.g., combining daily $P = 96$ with weekly $P = 672$ or multi-day $P = 192$) will outperform any single-period setting by capturing long-range inter-cycle drift.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Experimental Matrix and Controlled Variables\\n\\n### 2.1 Dataset Configuration & Preprocessing\\n- **Dataset**: ETTm1 (7 multivariate time series channels).\\n- **Partitioning**: Chronological split standard for benchmark comparability:\\n  - **Train**: First 12 months (34,560 time steps).\\n  - **Validation**: Subsequent 4 months (11,520 time steps).\\n  - **Test**: Final 4 months (11,520 time steps).\\n- **Data Scaling**: Z-score standard normalization ($\\\\mathbf{x}_{\\\\text{norm}} = \\\\frac{\\\\mathbf{x} - \\\\boldsymbol{\\\\mu}_{\\\\text{train}}}{\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\sigma}_{\\\\text{train}} + \\\\epsilon}$), fitted strictly on the training partition to prevent lookahead data leakage.\\n- **Task Modes**: Multivariate forecasting (predicting all 7 channels simultaneously) and Target Channel evaluation (measuring 'OT' accuracy).\\n\\n### 2.2 Independent Variable: Period Configurations ($P$)\\nTo systematically explore the performance landscape, 12 discrete period configurations grouped into 4 distinct operational categories are tested:\\n\\n| Group | Configuration ID | Period Setting ($P$) | Theoretical Rationale |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **A. Physical Harmonics** | `P-96` *(Baseline)* | $P = 96$ | True ground-truth diurnal cycle (24 hours). |\\n| | `P-48` | $P = 48$ | Sub-harmonic (12 hours / half-day). |\\n| | `P-192` | $P = 192$ | Super-harmonic (48 hours / 2 days). |\\n| | `P-24` | $P = 24$ | Hourly-dataset transfer baseline (6 hours on ETTm1). |\\n| **B. Non-Harmonic Controls** | `P-32` | $P = 32$ | Incommensurate sub-daily (8 hours). |\\n| | `P-64` | $P = 64$ | Incommensurate sub-daily (16 hours). |\\n| | `P-80` | $P = 80$ | Near-daily incommensurate (20 hours; test sensitivity near $P=96$). |\\n| | `P-112` | $P = 112$ | Near-daily incommensurate (28 hours; test sensitivity near $P=96$). |\\n| | `P-144` | $P = 144$ | Arbitrary 1.5-day cycle (36 hours). |\\n| **C. Multi-Period Combinations** | `P-Multi-A` | $[48, 96]$ | Dual-scale: half-day + full-day hierarchy. |\\n| *(if multi-period supported)* | `P-Multi-B` | $[96, 192]$ | Dual-scale: full-day + two-day hierarchy. |\\n| | `P-Multi-C` | $[24, 48, 96]$ | Triple-scale hierarchical decomposition. |\\n| **D. Data-Driven Baseline** | `P-Auto` | Top-$k$ FFT / AutoCorr | Unsupervised periodicity extracted from training set. |\\n\\n### 2.3 Prediction Horizons ($T$) and Lookback Window ($L$)\\n- **Lookback Context Window ($L$)**: Held fixed at $L = 336$ time steps (3.5 days), allowing sufficient historical cycles for all $P \\\\le 192$.\\n- **Ablation Context Window**: $L = 672$ evaluated specifically when testing weekly periodicity $P = 672$.\\n- **Prediction Horizons ($T$)**: Evaluated across the 4 standard long-term benchmarks:\\n  $$T \\\\in \\\\{96, 192, 336, 720\\\\} \\\\text{ steps (corresponding to 24h, 48h, 84h, and 180h)}$$\\n\\n### 2.4 Controlled Variables\\nTo isolate the effect of period settings, all other architectural and optimization parameters are held strictly invariant:\\n- **Model Architecture**: Number of layers ($N = 2$), model feature dimension ($d_{model} = 64$ or default PDF dimension), dropout rate ($p = 0.1$).\\n- **Optimizer**: Adam ($\\\\beta_1 = 0.9, \\\\beta_2 = 0.999$, weight decay $= 10^{-4}$).\\n- **Learning Rate Schedule**: Initial learning rate $\\\\eta = 10^{-3}$, reduced via Cosine Annealing or ReduceLROnPlateau (factor $0.5$, patience $2$).\\n- **Batch Size**: 32.\\n- **Maximum Epochs & Early Stopping**: 20 epochs with early stopping on validation loss (patience $= 5$ epochs).\\n- **Loss Function**: Mean Squared Error (MSE).\\n- **Random Seeds**: 5 independent fixed random seeds ($\\\\{2021, 2022, 2023, 2024, 2025\\\\}$) across every experimental cell.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Detailed Protocol and Evaluation Metrics\\n\\n### 3.1 Step-by-Step Execution Protocol\\n1. **Window Generation**: Construct input-target pairs $(\\\\mathbf{X}_{t-L+1:t}, \\\\mathbf{Y}_{t+1:t+T})$ using a rolling sliding window with step size 1 across the normalized split partitions.\\n2. **Model Instantiation**: Initialize the PDF model with the designated period setting $P$ from the experimental matrix, fixing all other hyperparameters.\\n3. **Training & Checkpointing**:\\n   - Train on the training split using backpropagation with the MSE loss.\\n   - Evaluate validation loss after each epoch. Save the model checkpoint that achieves the lowest validation MSE.\\n   - Terminate training if validation MSE does not improve for 5 consecutive epochs.\\n4. **Inference & Inverse Transformation**:\\n   - Run inference on the unseen test split using the optimal checkpoint.\\n   - Invert the standard normalization back to physical units: $\\\\hat{\\\\mathbf{Y}} = \\\\hat{\\\\mathbf{Y}}_{\\\\text{norm}} \\\\times \\\\boldsymbol{\\\\sigma}_{\\\\text{train}} + \\\\boldsymbol{\\\\mu}_{\\\\text{train}}$.\\n5. **Metric Calculation**: Compute primary and secondary metrics on both normalized and unnormalized scales.\\n\\n### 3.2 Evaluation Metrics\\n- **Mean Squared Error (MSE)**:\\n  $$\\\\text{MSE} = \\\\frac{1}{T \\\\cdot D} \\\\sum_{i=1}^T \\\\sum_{j=1}^D (Y_{i,j} - \\\\hat{Y}_{i,j})^2$$\\n- **Mean Absolute Error (MAE)**:\\n  $$\\\\text{MAE} = \\\\frac{1}{T \\\\cdot D} \\\\sum_{i=1}^T \\\\sum_{j=1}^D |Y_{i,j} - \\\\hat{Y}_{i,j}|$$\\n- **Relative Performance Change ($\\\\Delta \\\\text{Metric}_{\\\\%}$)** against the ground-truth baseline ($P = 96$):\\n  $$\\\\Delta \\\\text{MSE}_{\\\\%}(P) = \\\\frac{\\\\text{MSE}_P - \\\\text{MSE}_{P=96}}{\\\\text{MSE}_{P=96}} \\\\times 100\\\\%$$\\n- **Efficiency Metrics**: Average training wall-clock time per epoch (seconds), inference latency per batch (milliseconds), and peak GPU memory allocation (MB).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Statistical and Sensitivity Analysis Plan\\n\\n### 4.1 Statistical Aggregation & Significance Testing\\n- **Summary Statistics**: Report the sample mean ($\\\\bar{\\\\mu}$) and sample standard deviation ($\\\\sigma$) across the 5 independent random seed runs for each $(P, T)$ combination.\\n- **Hypothesis Testing**:\\n  - Perform **paired two-sided t-tests** and **Wilcoxon signed-rank tests** comparing each period setting against the ground-truth baseline ($P = 96$) across all test windows.\\n  - Apply the **Bonferroni-Holm adjustment** to control the family-wise error rate (FWER) across the multiple pairwise comparisons at significance threshold $\\\\alpha = 0.05$.\\n\\n### 4.2 Sensitivity Surface & Period Perturbation Analysis\\n- **Period-Error Response Curves**: Plot MSE/MAE as a continuous function of $P$ across horizons $T \\\\in \\\\{96, 192, 336, 720\\\\}$ to examine whether the loss surface forms a local convex minimum at $P = 96$.\\n- **Harmonic vs. Incommensurate Contrast**:\\n  - Compute contrast statistics:\\n    $$\\\\delta_{\\\\text{harmonic}} = \\\\text{MSE}_{\\\\text{harmonic}} - \\\\text{MSE}_{P=96}$$\\n    $$\\\\delta_{\\\\text{non-harmonic}} = \\\\text{MSE}_{\\\\text{non-harmonic}} - \\\\text{MSE}_{P=96}$$\\n  - Evaluate whether $\\\\delta_{\\\\text{harmonic}} < \\\\delta_{\\\\text{non-harmonic}}$ is statistically significant, confirming that integer-multiple phase alignment cushions performance loss.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Practical Feasibility, Computational Budget, and Validity Threats\\n\\n### 5.1 Computational Budget & Feasibility\\n- **Total Experimental Runs**:\\n  - $12 \\\\text{ period settings} \\\\times 4 \\\\text{ prediction horizons } (96, 192, 336, 720) \\\\times 5 \\\\text{ random seeds} = 240 \\\\text{ training runs}$.\\n- **Compute Estimation**:\\n  - ETTm1 training set has $34,560$ steps; with batch size 32 and early stopping at ~10\\u201315 epochs, each run requires approximately 3\\u20134 minutes on a single modern GPU (e.g., NVIDIA RTX 3090, A100, or V100).\\n  - Total computational cost is estimated at **12 to 16 GPU hours**, making the full matrix execution completely feasible within standard academic/industrial computing constraints.\\n\\n### 5.2 Potential Threats to Validity & Mitigation Strategies\\n1. **Window Divisibility and Boundary Padding**:\\n   - *Threat*: When the lookback window $L = 336$ is not an exact integer multiple of $P$ (e.g., $336 / 80 = 4.2$), models performing 2D reshaping must pad or truncate sequences, introducing edge artifacts.\\n   - *Mitigation*: Standardize the padding mechanism (reflective or circular padding rather than zero padding) and record padding overhead to separate architectural truncation artifacts from genuine periodic modeling differences.\\n2. **Hyperparameter Coupling**:\\n   - *Threat*: The optimal learning rate or dropout might vary slightly depending on the period setting (e.g., larger periods creating larger 2D representations).\\n   - *Mitigation*: Keep hyperparameters fixed at standard benchmark defaults to isolate the pure effect of the period prior, supplemented by a validation grid-search on learning rate $\\\\{10^{-4}, 10^{-3}\\\\}$ for the edge configurations ($P=24$ and $P=96$) to confirm that conclusions are not artifacts of sub-optimal learning rates.\\n3. **Temporal Covariate Shift**:\\n   - *Threat*: Seasonal variations across the 12/4/4 month splits (summer vs. winter transformer loads) could favor different periods in validation versus test sets.\\n   - *Mitigation*: Ensure models are selected strictly via validation loss and evaluate test metrics over the entire 4-month continuous test period, reporting rolling-window consistency.\\n4. **Non-Execution Notice**:\\n   - This document defines the complete experimental design, protocols, matrix, and analytical formulations. In strict accordance with the task instructions, no experimental execution or simulated results are claimed.\", \"assumptions\": [\"The PDF model accepts explicit period settings or period candidate lists as configuration hyperparameters.\", \"The standard ETTm1 benchmark split (12/4/4 months) and 15-minute sampling rate (96 steps/day) are used.\", \"Computational resources for executing the design require approximately 12-16 single-GPU hours for 240 training runs.\"], \"summary\": \"Complete experimental design for evaluating period settings of the PDF model on ETTm1\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "m-bUavywOp604_UPueOeEQ", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 23}
